# TLS server, CHUNKING reception with TLS shutdown mid-body and a byte in clear
gnutls
#
# The witness the sanitizer is driven with.  2038 and 2039 stop the session while
# the headers are still being read; this one gets past them, into the body, and
# then offers a byte in clear after the session has gone.  That last byte is what
# makes this the one of the three which reaches the released memory on a server
# without the fix, and having it reach that memory is the whole point: a
# reproducer which passes before the fix as well as after it proves nothing.
#
# What that byte does there.  Reading the body finds the session gone and, with
# nothing recorded to say so, falls through to the socket underneath - which is
# still open, so the read waits.  With nothing offered it waits until the receive
# timeout, which is where 2038 and 2039 end.  With one byte offered it returns,
# and that byte is taken as the last of the chunk: the chunk is then complete, the
# reader asks for what follows it, is told the message ended, and puts a character
# back to terminate a body that did not end with a line ending - through the
# reader belonging to the session that has gone, into the buffer released with it.
#
# Chunk arithmetic - 85 declared, 84 sent, so exactly one byte is outstanding when
# the session goes, and the byte offered in clear is that one.  The ">>> " prefix
# suppresses the trailing CRLF the client would otherwise add, so what is sent is
# exactly the characters after it: two header lines, the empty line ending them,
# and a body of 27 characters which does not end with a line ending.  Both of
# those last two properties matter.  Headers that are finished put the reader on
# the body path; a body that does not end with a line ending is what makes the
# reader put a character back at the end of the message.
#
# The reply is matched in full rather than as just "421" because the receive
# timeout answers with a 421 of its own, and a loose match would be satisfied by
# the very behaviour this test exists to reject.
#
# Unlike 2038 and 2039 the end of file is not asserted here, and cannot be: the
# byte offered in clear is never consumed, so the close carries a reset and the
# client is not able to read past the reply.  The reply and an empty queue are
# what is asserted instead - nothing may be accepted, the message being one byte
# short of what the peer said it was sending.
#
# Note for running this by hand: as for 2038 and 2039, the daemon under test has
# to be the GnuTLS build, so hand that binary to runtest and leave --tls off:
#
#     cd test && ./runtest <gnutls-binary> --continue --test 2040
#
# The reasons are given at length in 2038.  For the sanitizer run the harness is
# not used at all - it makes a setuid copy of the binary, which a sanitized build
# does not tolerate - so the client block below is replayed against a
# non-setuid instrumented daemon started directly.  See the Verification section
# of doc/doc-txt/exim-security-2026-07-31.1/report.txt.
#
exim -DSERVER=server -DSRV=tls -bd -oX PORT_D
****
#
client-anytls 127.0.0.1 PORT_D
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250-STARTTLS
??? 250 HELP
STARTTLS
??? 220
EHLO rhu.barb
??? 250-
??? 250-SIZE
??? 250-8BITMIME
??? 250-PIPELINING
??? 250-CHUNKING
??? 250 HELP
MAIL FROM:<someone@some.domain>
??? 250
RCPT TO:<CALLER@test.ex>
??? 250
BDAT 85 LAST
>>> To: Susan@random.com\r\nFrom: Sam@random.com\r\n\r\nbody-start-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
stoptls
>>> X
??? 421 Lost incoming connection
****
millisleep 500
killdaemon
